"use strict";
// Each example includes its own inputs, calculation and interpretation.
const data = [
["Financial Statement Analysis","Activity ratios","Working capital turnover","Revenue ÷ average working capital","Working capital = current assets − current liabilities. Average the beginning and ending working capital.","Measures revenue generated per unit of average working capital. Interpret cautiously when working capital is near zero or negative.","Revenue = $600; beginning working capital = $80; ending working capital = $120.\nAverage working capital = ($80 + $120) ÷ 2 = $100.\nTurnover = $600 ÷ $100 = 6.0×. The firm generates $6 of revenue per $1 of average working capital."],
["Financial Statement Analysis","Activity ratios","Inventory turnover","Cost of goods sold ÷ average inventory","Average inventory = (beginning inventory + ending inventory) ÷ 2.","How many times inventory is sold during the period; compare with peers and history.","COGS = $400; beginning inventory = $90; ending inventory = $110.\nTurnover = $400 ÷ $100 = 4.0×. Inventory turns four times per year."],
["Financial Statement Analysis","Activity ratios","Days of inventory on hand","365 ÷ inventory turnover","Use the number of days in the period if it differs from 365.","Estimated average number of days inventory is held.","Inventory turnover = 4× per year.\nDays of inventory = 365 ÷ 4 = 91.25 days. Inventory is held for approximately 91 days."],
["Financial Statement Analysis","Activity ratios","Receivables turnover","Revenue ÷ average trade receivables","Some questions use credit sales rather than total revenue; follow the given definition.","How quickly receivables are collected, expressed as turns per period.","Revenue = $900; average receivables = $150.\nTurnover = $900 ÷ $150 = 6×. Receivables turn six times annually."],
["Financial Statement Analysis","Activity ratios","Days sales outstanding","365 ÷ receivables turnover","Use the turnover rate for the same annual period.","Approximate average collection period.","Receivables turnover = 6×.\nDSO = 365 ÷ 6 = 60.8 days. Collection takes about 61 days."],
["Financial Statement Analysis","Activity ratios","Payables turnover","Purchases ÷ average trade payables","When purchases are not given, purchases = COGS + ending inventory − beginning inventory.","How often trade payables are paid during the period.","Purchases = $500; average payables = $100.\nTurnover = $500 ÷ $100 = 5×. Payables turn five times per year."],
["Financial Statement Analysis","Activity ratios","Cash conversion cycle","Days inventory + days receivables − days payables","Days payables = 365 ÷ payables turnover. Use consistent period lengths.","Time between paying suppliers and collecting cash from customers.","Inventory days = 80; receivable days = 40; payable days = 50.\nCash conversion cycle = 80 + 40 − 50 = 70 days. Cash is tied up for about 70 days."],
["Financial Statement Analysis","Activity ratios","Total asset turnover","Revenue ÷ average total assets","Use the average of beginning and ending total assets.","Revenue generated for each dollar invested in assets.","Revenue = $1,200; average assets = $800.\nTurnover = 1,200 ÷ 800 = 1.5×. Each dollar of assets generates $1.50 of revenue."],
["Financial Statement Analysis","Liquidity ratios","Current ratio","Current assets ÷ current liabilities","Both values come from the balance sheet at the same date.","A broad measure of short-term coverage, not a guarantee of liquidity.","Current assets = $240; current liabilities = $120.\nCurrent ratio = 240 ÷ 120 = 2.0×. There are $2 of current assets per $1 of current liabilities."],
["Financial Statement Analysis","Liquidity ratios","Quick ratio","(Cash + marketable securities + receivables) ÷ current liabilities","Exclude inventory and prepayments from these liquid assets.","Measures short-term coverage using relatively liquid assets.","Cash = $30; securities = $20; receivables = $70; current liabilities = $100.\nQuick ratio = (30 + 20 + 70) ÷ 100 = 1.2×."],
["Financial Statement Analysis","Liquidity ratios","Cash ratio","(Cash + marketable securities) ÷ current liabilities","Use cash and short-term marketable securities.","A conservative measure of immediate liability coverage.","Cash = $30; securities = $20; current liabilities = $100.\nCash ratio = (30 + 20) ÷ 100 = 0.5×."],
["Financial Statement Analysis","Solvency ratios","Debt-to-equity ratio","Total debt ÷ total equity","Use the debt definition specified in the question.","Compares debt financing with the book value of equity.","Total debt = $300; equity = $200.\nDebt-to-equity = 300 ÷ 200 = 1.5×. Debt is 150% of equity."],
["Financial Statement Analysis","Solvency ratios","Interest coverage","EBIT ÷ interest expense","EBIT = earnings before interest and taxes; use the same period for both.","Indicates the extent operating earnings cover interest.","EBIT = $150; interest expense = $30.\nInterest coverage = 150 ÷ 30 = 5×. EBIT covers interest five times."],
["Financial Statement Analysis","Profitability ratios","Gross profit margin","(Revenue − cost of goods sold) ÷ revenue","Gross profit is revenue less cost of goods sold.","Proportion of revenue remaining after direct cost of sales.","Revenue = $500; COGS = $300.\nGross margin = (500 − 300) ÷ 500 = 40%. Each revenue dollar leaves $0.40 before other expenses."],
["Financial Statement Analysis","Profitability ratios","Net profit margin","Net income ÷ revenue","Net income is after expenses and taxes.","Fraction of revenue retained as net income.","Net income = $60; revenue = $600.\nNet margin = 60 ÷ 600 = 10%. The company earns $0.10 per revenue dollar."],
["Financial Statement Analysis","Profitability ratios","Return on assets","Net income ÷ average total assets","Use average total assets over the income period.","Measures income generated relative to the asset base.","Net income = $80; average assets = $1,000.\nROA = 80 ÷ 1,000 = 8%."],
["Financial Statement Analysis","Profitability ratios","Return on equity","Net income ÷ average equity","Use net income attributable to common shareholders and average common equity where appropriate.","Measures earnings relative to shareholders’ book equity.","Net income = $60; average equity = $400.\nROE = 60 ÷ 400 = 15%."],
["Financial Statement Analysis","Financial analysis techniques","Three-step DuPont ROE","Net profit margin × total asset turnover × equity multiplier","Equity multiplier = average total assets ÷ average equity.","Separates ROE into profitability, asset efficiency and financial leverage.","Net margin = 10%; asset turnover = 1.5×; equity multiplier = 2×.\nROE = 10% × 1.5 × 2 = 30%."],
["Financial Statement Analysis","Income statements","Basic earnings per share","(Net income − preferred dividends) ÷ weighted-average common shares","Weight shares by the fraction of the period outstanding.","Earnings attributable to each weighted-average common share.","Net income = $120; preferred dividends = $20; weighted-average shares = 50.\nBasic EPS = (120 − 20) ÷ 50 = $2 per share."],
["Financial Statement Analysis","Cash flow analysis","Free cash flow to the firm","CFO + interest paid × (1 − tax rate) − capital expenditure","This form assumes CFO includes interest paid and the interest tax shield is available. Adjust if interest is classified elsewhere.","Cash flow available to both debt and equity capital providers.","CFO = $200; interest paid = $20; tax rate = 25%; capex = $80.\nFCFF = 200 + 20 × (1 − 0.25) − 80 = $135."],
["Financial Statement Analysis","Cash flow analysis","Free cash flow to equity","CFO − capital expenditure + net borrowing","Net borrowing = new debt issued − debt repaid. CFO should reflect interest paid.","Cash flow available to common equity after investment and debt flows.","CFO = $200; capex = $80; new borrowing = $50; repayment = $20.\nFCFE = 200 − 80 + (50 − 20) = $150."],
["Financial Statement Analysis","Inventory analysis","LIFO to FIFO inventory","FIFO inventory = LIFO inventory + LIFO reserve","The LIFO reserve is FIFO inventory minus LIFO inventory.","Restates inventory for comparability when the reserve is provided.","LIFO inventory = $180; LIFO reserve = $20.\nFIFO inventory = 180 + 20 = $200."],
["Quantitative Methods","Rates and returns","Holding period return","(Ending price − beginning price + cash income) ÷ beginning price","Cash income includes dividends or interest received during the holding period.","Total return for the holding period; it is not automatically annualized.","Beginning price = $100; ending price = $108; dividend = $2.\nHPR = (108 − 100 + 2) ÷ 100 = 10%."],
["Quantitative Methods","Rates and returns","Arithmetic mean return","Sum of periodic returns ÷ number of periods","Use returns measured over equal-length periods.","Average one-period return; it does not capture compounding.","Annual returns = 10%, −5%, 7%.\nArithmetic mean = (10% − 5% + 7%) ÷ 3 = 4%."],
["Quantitative Methods","Rates and returns","Geometric mean return","[(1 + R₁)(1 + R₂)…(1 + Rₙ)]^(1/n) − 1","Use decimal returns over equal-length periods.","The constant compounded return that produces the same terminal wealth.","Returns = 10% and −10%.\nGeometric mean = (1.10 × 0.90)^(1/2) − 1 ≈ −0.50% per period."],
["Quantitative Methods","Rates and returns","Effective annual rate","(1 + stated annual rate ÷ m)^m − 1","m is the number of compounding periods per year.","Converts a stated annual rate into its effective compounded annual rate.","Stated annual rate = 12%; m = 12.\nEAR = (1 + 0.12 ÷ 12)^12 − 1 ≈ 12.68%."],
["Quantitative Methods","Time value of money","Future value","PV × (1 + r)^n","PV = present value; r = rate per period; n = number of periods.","Value of a single amount after compounding.","PV = $1,000; r = 5% annually; n = 2 years.\nFV = 1,000 × 1.05² = $1,102.50."],
["Quantitative Methods","Time value of money","Present value","FV ÷ (1 + r)^n","FV = future value; r and n must use the same period.","Today's value of a future cash amount at the stated discount rate.","FV = $1,210; r = 10%; n = 2 years.\nPV = 1,210 ÷ 1.10² = $1,000."],
["Quantitative Methods","Time value of money","Present value of an ordinary annuity","PMT × [1 − (1 + r)^(−n)] ÷ r","Equal payments occur at the end of each period; r is nonzero.","Present value of level periodic payments.","PMT = $100 per year; r = 10%; n = 2.\nPV = 100 × [1 − 1.10⁻²] ÷ 0.10 ≈ $173.55."],
["Quantitative Methods","Time value of money","Future value of an ordinary annuity","PMT × [(1 + r)^n − 1] ÷ r","Payments occur at each period end; r is nonzero.","Accumulated value at the end of the last payment period.","PMT = $100 per year; r = 10%; n = 2.\nFV = 100 × (1.10² − 1) ÷ 0.10 = $210."],
["Quantitative Methods","Time value of money","Present value of a perpetuity","PMT ÷ r","Constant payments start one period from today; r must be positive.","Present value of an indefinitely continuing level payment stream.","Annual payment = $50; discount rate = 5%.\nPV = 50 ÷ 0.05 = $1,000."],
["Quantitative Methods","Time value of money","Net present value","Σ [CFₜ ÷ (1 + r)^t]","Include the initial cash flow at t = 0 and use the required return per period.","Positive NPV means value added relative to the discount rate.","Initial outflow = $100; inflow after one year = $110; discount rate = 5%.\nNPV = −100 + 110 ÷ 1.05 ≈ $4.76."],
["Quantitative Methods","Statistical measures","Sample variance","Σ (xᵢ − x̄)² ÷ (n − 1)","x̄ is the sample mean; n is the sample size, greater than one.","Measures dispersion around the sample mean in squared units.","Observations = 2, 4, 6; mean = 4.\nSample variance = [(−2)² + 0² + 2²] ÷ 2 = 4."],
["Quantitative Methods","Statistical measures","Coefficient of variation","Standard deviation ÷ mean","The mean must be nonzero; comparison is most useful when means are positive.","Measures dispersion per unit of mean.","Mean return = 8%; standard deviation = 12%.\nCV = 12% ÷ 8% = 1.5."],
["Quantitative Methods","Probability","Conditional probability","P(A | B) = P(A and B) ÷ P(B)","P(B) must be positive.","Probability of A given that B has occurred.","P(A and B) = 0.12; P(B) = 0.30.\nP(A | B) = 0.12 ÷ 0.30 = 0.40 or 40%."],
["Quantitative Methods","Probability","Bayes’ formula","P(A | B) = P(B | A) × P(A) ÷ P(B)","P(B) is the total probability of B and must be positive.","Updates the probability of A after observing B.","P(A) = 0.20; P(B | A) = 0.80; P(B | not A) = 0.10.\nP(B) = 0.80 × 0.20 + 0.10 × 0.80 = 0.24.\nP(A | B) = 0.16 ÷ 0.24 ≈ 66.67%."],
["Quantitative Methods","Probability","Expected value","Σ [pᵢ × xᵢ]","The probabilities of mutually exclusive, exhaustive outcomes sum to 1.","Probability-weighted average outcome.","Returns: 20% with probability 0.4, and 5% with probability 0.6.\nExpected return = 0.4 × 20% + 0.6 × 5% = 11%."],
["Quantitative Methods","Portfolio mathematics","Two-asset portfolio expected return","E(Rₚ) = Σ wᵢE(Rᵢ); two assets: w₁E(R₁) + w₂E(R₂)","Weights sum to one for a fully invested portfolio. The same weighted sum applies to realized returns.","Weighted average of the assets' expected returns.","Weight A = 60%, return A = 10%; weight B = 40%, return B = 5%.\nExpected portfolio return = 0.6 × 10% + 0.4 × 5% = 8%."],
["Quantitative Methods","Portfolio mathematics","Two-asset portfolio variance","w₁²σ₁² + w₂²σ₂² + 2w₁w₂σ₁σ₂ρ₁₂","σ denotes standard deviation and ρ is correlation. The last term can also be written 2w₁w₂Cov(R₁,R₂). Use decimal returns.","Captures both individual risks and how the assets move together.","Weights = 0.5 each; σ₁ = 20%, σ₂ = 10%, correlation = 0.\nVariance = 0.5² × 0.2² + 0.5² × 0.1² = 0.0125.\nStandard deviation = √0.0125 ≈ 11.18%."],
["Quantitative Methods","Portfolio mathematics","Covariance from correlation","Cov(R₁,R₂) = ρ₁₂ × σ₁ × σ₂","Correlation is between −1 and +1; standard deviations use the same period.","Describes joint movements in return units squared.","Correlation = 0.5; standard deviations = 20% and 10%.\nCovariance = 0.5 × 0.20 × 0.10 = 0.01."],
["Quantitative Methods","Portfolio mathematics","Roy’s safety-first ratio","[E(Rₚ) − minimum acceptable return] ÷ σₚ","σₚ is portfolio standard deviation. For the probability interpretation, assume normally distributed returns.","Larger values imply a lower probability of falling below the threshold under normality.","Expected return = 10%; threshold = 4%; standard deviation = 12%.\nSafety-first ratio = (10% − 4%) ÷ 12% = 0.5."],
["Quantitative Methods","Estimation and inference","Standard error of the sample mean","s ÷ √n","s is sample standard deviation; n is sample size. Assumes independent observations.","Estimated standard deviation of the sample mean.","Sample standard deviation = 12%; sample size = 36.\nStandard error = 12% ÷ √36 = 2%."],
["Quantitative Methods","Hypothesis testing","Test statistic for a mean","(sample mean − hypothesized mean) ÷ standard error","For a one-sample t-statistic, the estimated standard error is s ÷ √n; choose the appropriate z or t distribution for the assumptions and available information.","How many standard errors the sample mean is from the hypothesized mean.","Sample mean = 8%; hypothesized mean = 6%; standard error = 1%.\nTest statistic = (8% − 6%) ÷ 1% = 2."],
["Quantitative Methods","Simple linear regression","Predicted value","ŷ = b₀ + b₁x","b₀ is the estimated intercept and b₁ is the estimated slope.","The model's predicted dependent variable for a given x.","Intercept = 2; slope = 3; x = 4.\nPredicted y = 2 + 3 × 4 = 14."],
["Economics","The firm and market structures","Accounting profit","Total revenue − explicit costs","Explicit costs are observable payments and expenses.","Profit after explicit costs, before deducting implicit opportunity costs.","Revenue = $500; explicit costs = $350.\nAccounting profit = 500 − 350 = $150."],
["Economics","The firm and market structures","Economic profit","Total revenue − explicit costs − implicit costs","Implicit costs include the opportunity cost of owner-supplied resources.","Profit after all opportunity costs; zero economic profit can still be a normal return.","Revenue = $500; explicit costs = $350; implicit costs = $100.\nEconomic profit = 500 − 350 − 100 = $50."],
["Economics","The firm and market structures","Breakeven quantity","Fixed costs ÷ (price per unit − variable cost per unit)","Assumes a constant selling price and unit variable cost; contribution margin must be positive.","Units needed for zero operating profit.","Fixed costs = $1,000; price = $20; variable cost = $15 per unit.\nBreakeven = 1,000 ÷ (20 − 15) = 200 units."],
["Economics","Business cycles","Unemployment rate","Number of unemployed ÷ labor force × 100%","Labor force = employed + unemployed; unemployment requires active job seeking.","Share of the labor force that is unemployed.","Unemployed = 5 million; employed = 95 million.\nUnemployment rate = 5 ÷ (95 + 5) = 5%."],
["Economics","Business cycles","Inflation rate","(Current price index − previous price index) ÷ previous price index","Use consistent price-index definitions.","Percentage change in the general price index over the period.","Prior CPI = 200; current CPI = 210.\nInflation = (210 − 200) ÷ 200 = 5%."],
["Economics","Fiscal policy","Fiscal multiplier (simple model)","1 ÷ (1 − MPC)","MPC = marginal propensity to consume. This is a simplified closed-economy model with no taxes or imports.","Change in equilibrium output per unit of autonomous spending under these assumptions.","MPC = 0.75.\nMultiplier = 1 ÷ (1 − 0.75) = 4. An increase of $10 in autonomous spending implies $40 more output in this model."],
["Economics","Monetary policy","Quantity equation of money","M × V = P × Y","M = money supply; V = velocity; P = price level; Y = real output. This is an identity.","Nominal output equals the money stock multiplied by its turnover rate.","M = $500 billion; V = 4; real output Y = 1,000 billion units.\nP = (500 × 4) ÷ 1,000 = $2 per unit."],
["Economics","Monetary policy","Fisher relation","1 + nominal rate = (1 + real rate) × (1 + expected inflation)","The common approximation is nominal rate ≈ real rate + expected inflation.","Relates nominal and real rates for a given expected inflation rate.","Real rate = 2%; expected inflation = 3%.\nNominal rate = 1.02 × 1.03 − 1 = 5.06%."],
["Economics","International trade","Trade balance","Exports − imports","Use exports and imports of goods and services for the trade balance definition in the question.","A positive value indicates a trade surplus; a negative value indicates a deficit.","Exports = $120 billion; imports = $150 billion.\nTrade balance = 120 − 150 = −$30 billion, a deficit."],
["Economics","Exchange rate calculations","Currency conversion","Amount in base currency × spot rate (price currency per base currency)","Identify the price currency in the numerator and the base currency in the denominator before multiplying.","Converts a known base-currency amount into the price currency.","Spot = 1.20 USD per EUR; amount = €100.\n$ amount = 100 EUR × 1.20 USD/EUR = $120."],
["Economics","Exchange rate calculations","Inverse exchange rate","1 ÷ spot rate","If the original rate is price currency per base currency, the reciprocal reverses the pair.","Converts a quote into the opposite currency quotation.","Spot = 1.20 USD/EUR.\nInverse = 1 ÷ 1.20 ≈ 0.8333 EUR/USD."],
["Economics","Exchange rate calculations","Forward exchange rate","F(price/base) = S(price/base) × (1 + r_price) ÷ (1 + r_base)","For one period and matching maturities, use simple period rates. Price currency is the numerator of the quote.","Covered interest parity links the forward rate to spot and the two interest rates; this is not a forecast.","Spot = 1.20 USD/EUR; one-year USD rate = 5%; EUR rate = 3%.\nForward = 1.20 × 1.05 ÷ 1.03 ≈ 1.2233 USD/EUR."],
["Economics","Exchange rate calculations","Forward premium or discount","(Forward rate − spot rate) ÷ spot rate","Use the same quote convention for both rates; this is for the contract period, not automatically annualized.","Positive means the base currency is at a forward premium relative to the price currency.","Spot = 1.20 USD/EUR; forward = 1.23 USD/EUR.\nPremium = (1.23 − 1.20) ÷ 1.20 = 2.5% for the contract period."],
["Corporate Issuers","Cost of capital","Weighted average cost of capital","wᴅrᴅ(1 − T) + wᴘrᴘ + wᴇrᴇ","Weights are market-value proportions of debt, preferred and common equity; T is the marginal tax rate and rᴅ is pretax debt cost. Assume interest is tax-deductible.","A blended required return for providers of capital; suitable for projects with similar risk and capital structure.","Debt: 30% at 6%; preferred: 10% at 8%; equity: 60% at 12%; tax rate: 25%.\nWACC = 0.30 × 6% × 0.75 + 0.10 × 8% + 0.60 × 12% = 9.35%."],
["Corporate Issuers","Cost of capital","CAPM cost of equity","Risk-free rate + equity beta × market risk premium","Market risk premium = expected market return − risk-free rate; beta measures systematic risk.","Estimates shareholders' required return for a given market risk exposure.","Risk-free rate = 3%; beta = 1.2; expected market return = 8%.\nCost of equity = 3% + 1.2 × (8% − 3%) = 9%."],
["Corporate Issuers","Cost of capital","Dividend growth cost of equity","D₁ ÷ P₀ + g","D₁ is next period's expected dividend; P₀ is current price; g is a constant perpetual growth rate.","Rearranges the Gordon growth model to estimate the required return.","Next dividend = $2; current price = $40; growth = 4%.\nCost of equity = 2 ÷ 40 + 4% = 9%."],
["Corporate Issuers","Cost of capital","After-tax cost of debt","Pretax cost of debt × (1 − tax rate)","Assumes interest is fully deductible and the tax shield can be used.","Reflects the reduction in effective borrowing cost from the interest tax shield.","Pretax cost = 8%; tax rate = 25%.\nAfter-tax cost = 8% × 0.75 = 6%."],
["Corporate Issuers","Capital investment","Internal rate of return","0 = Σ [CFₜ ÷ (1 + IRR)^t]","IRR is the discount rate that makes NPV zero. Multiple IRRs or no IRR may occur for nonconventional cash flows. When CFs are the investor's dated portfolio contributions, distributions and ending value, the result is the money-weighted rate of return (MWRR).","For a conventional project's IRR, compare with its required return and use NPV for conflicting rankings. MWRR measures the investor's experience and is affected by the timing and size of external cash flows.","Pay $100 today and receive $110 in one year.\n0 = −100 + 110 ÷ (1 + IRR), so IRR = 10%. The same equation gives 10% MWRR when these are an investor's cash flows."],
["Corporate Issuers","Capital investment","Profitability index","Present value of future cash inflows ÷ initial investment","Initial investment is a positive amount; use the required return to discount inflows.","For a conventional independent project, an index above 1 corresponds to positive NPV.","PV of future inflows = $120; initial investment = $100.\nPI = 120 ÷ 100 = 1.20; NPV = $20."],
["Corporate Issuers","Capital investment","Payback period","Years before recovery + unrecovered amount ÷ next year's cash flow","Assumes the next year's cash flow occurs evenly over that year. This is the undiscounted payback.","Measures how quickly initial cash outlay is recovered, ignoring time value and later flows.","Initial outlay = $100; year 1 inflow = $60; year 2 inflow = $80.\nPayback = 1 + (100 − 60) ÷ 80 = 1.5 years."],
["Corporate Issuers","Leverage","Degree of operating leverage","Percentage change in operating income ÷ percentage change in sales","At a given output with constant unit price and variable cost, DOL = contribution margin ÷ operating income.","Measures the sensitivity of operating income to sales; fixed operating costs increase operating leverage.","Sales increase by 10% and operating income increases by 25%.\nDOL = 25% ÷ 10% = 2.5."],
["Corporate Issuers","Leverage","Degree of financial leverage","Percentage change in net income ÷ percentage change in operating income","For a simple capital structure without preferred dividends, DFL = EBIT ÷ (EBIT − interest).","Measures the sensitivity of net income to operating income due to financing costs.","EBIT = $100; interest = $20.\nDFL = 100 ÷ (100 − 20) = 1.25."],
["Corporate Issuers","Working capital","Net working capital","Current assets − current liabilities","Use balance-sheet values at the same date; some investment calculations use operating working capital instead.","A basic measure of short-term net resources.","Current assets = $250; current liabilities = $150.\nNet working capital = 250 − 150 = $100."],
["Equity Investments","Dividend discount models","Gordon growth share value","P₀ = D₁ ÷ (r − g)","D₁ is next period's dividend; r is required return; g is constant perpetual dividend growth; r must exceed g. The general growing perpetuity formula is PV = CF₁ ÷ (r − g).","Value of a share assuming dividends grow indefinitely at a constant rate; the same formula values other perpetually growing cash flows.","Next dividend = $2; required return = 9%; growth = 4%.\nP₀ = 2 ÷ (0.09 − 0.04) = $40."],
["Equity Investments","Dividend discount models","Next dividend","D₁ = D₀ × (1 + g)","D₀ is the dividend just paid; g is the expected growth rate for the next period.","Projects the next dividend used in a dividend discount model.","Most recent dividend = $2; growth = 5%.\nD₁ = 2 × 1.05 = $2.10."],
["Equity Investments","Dividend discount models","Sustainable growth rate","Retention ratio × return on equity","Retention ratio = 1 − dividend payout ratio. Assumes stable ROE and reinvestment without new equity issuance.","Estimates the growth supported by retained earnings under these assumptions.","Dividend payout = 40%; ROE = 15%.\nGrowth = (1 − 0.40) × 15% = 9%."],
["Equity Investments","Dividend discount models","Dividend payout ratio","Dividend per share ÷ earnings per share","Use dividends and earnings attributable to common shareholders for the same period.","The fraction of earnings paid to shareholders as dividends.","Dividend per share = $2; EPS = $5.\nPayout ratio = 2 ÷ 5 = 40%."],
["Equity Investments","Market multiples","Trailing price-to-earnings ratio","Market price per share ÷ trailing EPS","Use the last 12 months of earnings per common share. A negative EPS makes the ratio difficult to interpret.","Shows the price paid per unit of historical earnings; compare consistent accounting and growth assumptions.","Share price = $60; trailing EPS = $4.\nTrailing P/E = 60 ÷ 4 = 15×."],
["Equity Investments","Market multiples","Forward price-to-earnings ratio","Market price per share ÷ expected next-period EPS","Use expected EPS for the stated forward period.","Price relative to expected earnings rather than historical earnings.","Share price = $60; forecast next-year EPS = $5.\nForward P/E = 60 ÷ 5 = 12×."],
["Equity Investments","Market multiples","Price-to-book ratio","Market price per share ÷ book value per share","Book value per share = common shareholders' equity ÷ common shares outstanding.","Compares market value with accounting equity; interpret cautiously if book equity is negative.","Share price = $30; book value per share = $20.\nP/B = 30 ÷ 20 = 1.5×."],
["Equity Investments","Market multiples","Enterprise value","Market value of equity + total debt + preferred stock + noncontrolling interest − cash","Use consistent market-value inputs where available and the definition required by the question.","Approximate value of operating assets available to all capital providers.","Equity = $500; debt = $200; preferred = $20; noncontrolling interest = $10; cash = $30.\nEV = 500 + 200 + 20 + 10 − 30 = $700."],
["Equity Investments","Market multiples","Enterprise value to EBITDA","Enterprise value ÷ EBITDA","EBITDA = earnings before interest, taxes, depreciation and amortization; use matching periods and scope.","A valuation multiple that is less affected by financing structure than P/E, but is not a cash flow measure.","EV = $700; EBITDA = $100.\nEV/EBITDA = 700 ÷ 100 = 7×."],
["Equity Investments","Returns","Dividend yield","Annual dividend per share ÷ current share price","Use a stated trailing or expected annual dividend consistently.","Income component relative to the price paid; it excludes price changes.","Annual dividend = $2; share price = $50.\nDividend yield = 2 ÷ 50 = 4%."],
["Fixed Income","Bond valuation","Fixed-rate bond price","Σ [couponₜ ÷ (1 + y)^t] + principal ÷ (1 + y)^n","Use yield per coupon period and the number of coupon periods; assumes a flat discount rate and scheduled payments. Yield to maturity is the y that makes this present value equal the observed bond price; with spot rates, discount each cash flow at its matching spot rate.","Present value of promised coupons and principal; price falls as yield rises, all else equal. Solving this price equation for y gives yield to maturity, so a separate YTM card is unnecessary.","Face value = $100; annual coupon = $5; maturity = 2 years; annual yield = 10%.\nPrice = 5 ÷ 1.10 + 105 ÷ 1.10² ≈ $91.32."],
["Fixed Income","Bond valuation","Zero-coupon bond price","Face value ÷ (1 + y)^n; by spot curve: CFₙ ÷ (1 + sₙ)^n","y is the yield per period; sₙ is the n-period spot rate, which discounts a single cash flow at time n.","Present value of principal with no interim coupons. Discounting each bond cash flow at its matching spot rate extends this to a spot curve.","Face value = $1,000; annual yield = 5%; maturity = 2 years.\nPrice = 1,000 ÷ 1.05² ≈ $907.03."],
["Fixed Income","Bond valuation","Full price","Flat price + accrued interest","Full price is also called dirty price; flat price is the quoted clean price.","The buyer's settlement payment includes interest accrued since the previous coupon.","Flat price = $980; accrued interest = $12.\nFull price = 980 + 12 = $992."],
["Fixed Income","Bond valuation","Accrued interest","Coupon payment × elapsed days ÷ days in coupon period","The applicable day-count convention determines the elapsed days and period length.","Allocates the current coupon between seller and buyer for settlement.","Coupon = $30; elapsed fraction = 60 ÷ 180.\nAccrued interest = 30 × 60 ÷ 180 = $10."],
["Fixed Income","Yield measures","Current yield","Annual coupon payment ÷ flat bond price","It excludes capital gains and losses and is not yield to maturity.","Coupon income relative to the current clean price.","Annual coupon = $50; flat price = $950.\nCurrent yield = 50 ÷ 950 ≈ 5.26%."],
["Fixed Income","Yield measures","Bond-equivalent yield","Periodic yield × coupon periods per year","A nominal annualized yield; it does not compound interim periods.","Allows comparison using a stated annual yield with the same compounding frequency.","Semiannual yield = 3%.\nBond-equivalent yield = 3% × 2 = 6%."],
["Fixed Income","Interest rate risk","Approximate modified / effective duration","(P₋ − P₊) ÷ (2 × P₀ × Δy)","P₋ and P₊ are prices for equal yield decreases and increases; Δy is the yield change as a decimal. For effective duration, reprice allowing option-sensitive cash flows to change.","Estimates percentage price sensitivity to a small parallel yield shift. The same finite-difference formula is called effective duration when cash flows may change; this avoids duplicating the equation on another card.","P₋ = 102; P₊ = 98; P₀ = 100; Δy = 0.01.\nDuration = (102 − 98) ÷ (2 × 100 × 0.01) = 2 years."],
["Fixed Income","Interest rate risk","Duration price change","Approximate percentage price change = −modified duration × Δy","Δy is a decimal yield change; this linear estimate ignores convexity.","Approximates price movement for a small yield change, in the opposite direction.","Modified duration = 4; yield rises by 0.5 percentage point = 0.005.\nApproximate price change = −4 × 0.005 = −2%."],
["Fixed Income","Interest rate risk","Approximate convexity","(P₋ + P₊ − 2P₀) ÷ (P₀ × Δy²)","Use equal yield changes as decimals and consistent prices. This finite-difference convention omits an extra factor of two.","Captures curvature of the price–yield relationship, complementing duration.","P₋ = 102.1; P₊ = 98.1; P₀ = 100; Δy = 0.01.\nConvexity = (102.1 + 98.1 − 200) ÷ (100 × 0.01²) = 20."],
["Fixed Income","Interest rate risk","Duration and convexity price change","Approximate ΔP/P = −duration × Δy + ½ × convexity × (Δy)²","Duration and convexity must use the same yield and compounding conventions; Δy is a decimal.","Adds a curvature adjustment to the linear duration estimate.","Duration = 4; convexity = 20; yield increase = 0.01.\nΔP/P ≈ −4 × 0.01 + 0.5 × 20 × 0.01² = −3.9%."],
["Fixed Income","Yield curves","Forward rate from spot rates","(1 + z₂)² ÷ (1 + z₁) − 1","z₁ and z₂ are one- and two-year annual spot rates with annual compounding. The result is the one-year rate beginning in one year.","The no-arbitrage rate implied by two spot rates; it is not necessarily a forecast.","One-year spot = 4%; two-year spot = 5%.\nOne-year forward = 1.05² ÷ 1.04 − 1 ≈ 6.01%."],
["Derivatives","Forward commitments","Long forward payoff at expiry","Sₜ − K","Sₜ is the underlying price at expiration and K is the contracted delivery price; no initial premium is included.","A long forward gains when the underlying finishes above the delivery price.","Expiration price = $120; delivery price = $100.\nLong payoff = 120 − 100 = $20 per unit."],
["Derivatives","Forward commitments","Short forward payoff at expiry","K − Sₜ","K is the contracted delivery price. This is the opposite of the long forward payoff.","A short forward gains when the underlying finishes below the delivery price.","Expiration price = $120; delivery price = $100.\nShort payoff = 100 − 120 = −$20 per unit."],
["Derivatives","Forward pricing","Forward price without income","Discrete annual: F₀(T) = S₀(1 + r)^T · Continuous: F₀(T) = S₀e^(rT)","S₀ is spot price; r is the continuously or annually compounded risk-free rate as indicated; T is years. Assume no income, storage cost or arbitrage.","The no-arbitrage delivery price equals the financed spot price; choose the formula matching the stated compounding convention.","Spot price = $100; risk-free rate = 5%; T = 1 year.\nAnnual compounding: 100 × 1.05 = $105.00.\nContinuous compounding: 100 × e^0.05 ≈ $105.13."],
["Derivatives","Forward pricing","Forward price with known income","F₀(T) = [S₀ + PV(carry costs) − PV(benefits)] × (1 + r)^T","Discount known costs and benefits occurring during the contract to time zero; assume annual compounding. Known income is a benefit.","The no-arbitrage forward price increases with carrying costs and decreases with income or other benefits.","Spot = $100; PV of income = $2; no other costs; risk-free rate = 5%; T = 1 year.\nForward price = [100 − 2] × 1.05 = $102.90."],
["Derivatives","Option payoffs","Long call payoff","max(0, Sₜ − K)","Sₜ is the underlying price at expiration; K is strike. Payoff excludes the premium.","The call holder benefits from price increases above the strike and can let the option expire. At expiration, this payoff is the call's intrinsic value.","Expiration price = $120; strike = $100.\nCall payoff (intrinsic value at expiration) = max(0, 120 − 100) = $20 per unit."],
["Derivatives","Option payoffs","Long put payoff","max(0, K − Sₜ)","Sₜ is the underlying price at expiration; K is strike. Payoff excludes the premium.","The put holder benefits from price declines below the strike. At expiration, this payoff is the put's intrinsic value.","Expiration price = $80; strike = $100.\nPut payoff (intrinsic value at expiration) = max(0, 100 − 80) = $20 per unit."],
["Derivatives","Option payoffs","Long option profit at expiration","Option payoff − initial premium","This simple profit ignores financing cost; compare the premium and payoff per unit.","A positive payoff can still produce a loss if it is smaller than the premium.","Call payoff = $20; initial premium = $6.\nProfit = 20 − 6 = $14 per unit."],
["Derivatives","Option valuation","European put–call parity","S₀ + p₀ = c₀ + PV(K)","For European options on a non-dividend-paying asset with the same strike and expiration; assume no arbitrage.","Connects call, put, spot and the present value of the strike through replication.","Spot = $100; put = $5; PV of strike = $95.\nCall = 100 + 5 − 95 = $10."],
["Derivatives","Forward rate agreements","FRA settlement for the long","Notional × (reference rate − contract rate) × δ ÷ (1 + reference rate × δ)","Long is the fixed-rate payer. δ is the year fraction; settlement occurs at the start of the underlying loan period and uses simple rates.","Discounts the difference between the reference and fixed rates to settlement.","Notional = $1,000,000; reference = 6%; contract = 5%; δ = 0.5.\nSettlement = 1,000,000 × (0.06 − 0.05) × 0.5 ÷ (1 + 0.06 × 0.5) ≈ $4,854.37 paid to the long."],
["Alternative Investments","Real estate","Net operating income","Effective rental income + other operating income − operating expenses","Deduct vacancy and collection losses before effective rental income. Exclude financing costs, income taxes and capital expenditure.","Measures operating income produced by a property before financing.","Potential rent = $120,000; vacancy = $10,000; other income = $5,000; operating expenses = $35,000.\nNOI = 120,000 − 10,000 + 5,000 − 35,000 = $80,000."],
["Alternative Investments","Real estate","Capitalization rate","Net operating income ÷ property value","Use the NOI and property value for the same valuation date and the convention specified.","A property's unlevered income yield relative to its value.","NOI = $80,000; value = $1,000,000.\nCap rate = 80,000 ÷ 1,000,000 = 8%."],
["Alternative Investments","Real estate","Direct capitalization property value","NOI ÷ capitalization rate","Use a suitable stabilized forward NOI and a market-derived cap rate; cap rate must be positive.","Values a property by capitalizing one period of operating income.","Forward NOI = $80,000; cap rate = 8%.\nProperty value = 80,000 ÷ 0.08 = $1,000,000."],
["Alternative Investments","Private capital performance","Distribution to paid-in capital","Cumulative distributions ÷ paid-in capital","Use cumulative capital contributions as the denominator.","Realized cash distributions relative to contributed capital; ignores timing.","Distributions = $60 million; paid-in capital = $100 million.\nDPI = 60 ÷ 100 = 0.60×."],
["Alternative Investments","Private capital performance","Residual value to paid-in capital","Residual value ÷ paid-in capital","Residual value is the current estimated value of remaining fund investments.","Unrealized remaining value relative to contributed capital; ignores timing.","Residual value = $90 million; paid-in capital = $100 million.\nRVPI = 90 ÷ 100 = 0.90×."],
["Alternative Investments","Private capital performance","Total value to paid-in capital","(Cumulative distributions + residual value) ÷ paid-in capital = DPI + RVPI","Use consistent fund-level values and fee basis. It is an investment multiple, not an annualized return.","Combines realized and remaining value per unit of capital contributed.","Distributions = $60 million; residual value = $90 million; paid-in capital = $100 million.\nTVPI = (60 + 90) ÷ 100 = 1.50×."],
["Alternative Investments","Fund fees","Management fee","Management fee rate × specified fee base","The base may be committed capital, invested capital, or NAV; follow the fund terms and period.","The recurring fee before any incentive fee.","Annual fee = 2%; specified fee base = $100 million.\nManagement fee = 2% × $100 million = $2 million."],
["Alternative Investments","Fund fees","High-water-mark incentive fee","Incentive rate × max(0, ending fee-base NAV − high-water mark)","A simplified no-flow, no-hurdle example. The contract determines the fee base, timing and treatment of management fees.","Charges an incentive fee only on gains above the prior high-water mark, under the stated terms.","High-water mark = $120 million; ending fee-base NAV = $130 million; incentive rate = 20%.\nFee = 20% × max(0, 130 − 120) = $2 million."],
["Alternative Investments","Fund fees","Hard-hurdle incentive fee","Incentive rate × max(0, profit − hurdle amount)","A simplified hard hurdle with no catch-up; check whether profit is before or after management fees and other fund-specific terms.","The incentive fee applies only to the profit above the specified hurdle.","Profit = $15 million; hurdle amount = $8 million; incentive rate = 20%.\nFee = 20% × (15 − 8) = $1.4 million."],
["Quantitative Methods","Time value of money","Future value with continuous compounding","FV = PV × e^(r꜀ × t)","PV is the present value; r꜀ is the continuously compounded annual rate; t is time in years and e is approximately 2.71828.","Compounds a present amount continuously over time.","PV = $1,000; continuously compounded annual rate = 5%; t = 2 years.\nFV = 1,000 × e^(0.05 × 2) ≈ $1,105.17."],
["Quantitative Methods","Rates and returns","Continuously compounded rate","r꜀ = ln(1 + effective periodic return)","ln is the natural logarithm. The result is a continuously compounded rate for the same period; the effective return must be greater than −100%.","Converts an effective return to its equivalent continuously compounded rate for the same period.","Effective annual return = 10%.\nContinuously compounded annual rate = ln(1.10) ≈ 9.53%."],
["Quantitative Methods","Time value of money","Present value of a growing annuity","PV = PMT₁ ÷ (r − g) × [1 − ((1 + g) ÷ (1 + r))^n]","PMT₁ is the first payment at the end of period 1; payments grow by g each period for n periods. Rates are per period; this form requires r ≠ g and r > −1.","Discounts a finite series of growing payments; when r = g, PV = n × PMT₁ ÷ (1 + r).","First annual payment = $100; growth = 5%; discount rate = 10%; n = 2 years.\nPV = 100 ÷ (0.10 − 0.05) × [1 − (1.05 ÷ 1.10)²] ≈ $177.69.\nEquivalently, 100 ÷ 1.10 + 105 ÷ 1.10² ≈ $177.69."]
];
data.push(
["Economics","Macroeconomic aggregates","Expenditure approach to GDP","GDP = C + I + G + (X − M)","C = consumption; I = gross investment; G = government purchases; X = exports; M = imports.","Measures domestic production by summing spending on final goods and services.","C = $1,000bn; I = $300bn; G = $250bn; X = $100bn; M = $150bn.\nGDP = 1,000 + 300 + 250 + (100 − 150) = $1,500bn."],
["Economics","Inflation and interest rates","Exact Fisher relation","1 + nominal rate = (1 + real rate)(1 + expected inflation)","All rates cover the same period. For small rates, nominal rate ≈ real rate + expected inflation.","The exact multiplicative relation between nominal rates, real rates and expected inflation.","Real rate = 2%; expected inflation = 3%.\nNominal rate = 1.02 × 1.03 − 1 = 5.06%."],
["Economics","International economics","Relative purchasing power parity","Expected %Δ(spot, domestic per foreign) ≈ inflation_domestic − inflation_foreign","Spot is quoted as units of domestic currency per unit of foreign currency; the approximation assumes the rates apply over the same period.","Links expected exchange-rate change to inflation differentials under relative PPP.","Domestic inflation = 5%; foreign inflation = 2%.\nExpected change in the domestic-per-foreign quote ≈ 5% − 2% = +3%."],
["Economics","Demand and supply","Price elasticity of demand","%Δ quantity demanded ÷ %Δ own price","Use the percentage-change convention specified by the question; elasticity is usually negative for a downward-sloping demand curve.","Measures how responsive quantity demanded is to a change in its own price.","Price rises 10%; quantity demanded falls 20%.\nPrice elasticity = −20% ÷ 10% = −2. Demand is elastic."],
["Economics","Demand and supply","Cross-price elasticity of demand","%Δ quantity demanded of X ÷ %Δ price of Y","Use consistent percentage-change conventions.","Positive values generally indicate substitutes; negative values generally indicate complements.","Price of Y rises 5%; demand for X rises 10%.\nCross-price elasticity = 10% ÷ 5% = 2; X and Y are substitutes."],
["Economics","Demand and supply","Income elasticity of demand","%Δ quantity demanded ÷ %Δ consumer income","Use consistent percentage-change conventions.","Positive values generally indicate normal goods; negative values indicate inferior goods.","Income rises 4%; demand rises 6%.\nIncome elasticity = 6% ÷ 4% = 1.5; demand is income-elastic."],
["Economics","Monetary policy","Simple deposit multiplier","1 ÷ required reserve ratio","A simplified maximum multiple assuming no currency leakage and no excess reserves.","The theoretical maximum change in bank deposits per unit of reserves in the simple model.","Required reserve ratio = 10%.\nDeposit multiplier = 1 ÷ 0.10 = 10×."],
["Economics","Macroeconomic aggregates","Real GDP from nominal GDP","Real GDP = nominal GDP ÷ (GDP deflator ÷ 100)","Assumes the GDP deflator is an index with base value 100.","Removes the price-level effect from nominal GDP to measure output in base-period prices.","Nominal GDP = $2,100bn; GDP deflator = 105.\nReal GDP = 2,100 ÷ 1.05 = $2,000bn."],
["Economics","Business cycles","Output gap","Actual GDP − potential GDP","The absolute gap is in currency units. As a percentage: [(actual GDP − potential GDP) ÷ potential GDP] × 100%.","A positive gap indicates output above potential; a negative gap indicates spare capacity relative to potential.","Actual GDP = $980bn; potential GDP = $1,000bn.\nOutput gap = −$20bn, or −2% of potential GDP."],
["Financial Statement Analysis","Solvency ratios","Debt-to-assets ratio","Total debt ÷ total assets","Use the debt definition specified by the question.","Shows the proportion of assets financed by debt.","Total debt = $300; total assets = $750.\nDebt-to-assets = 300 ÷ 750 = 40%."],
["Corporate Issuers","Leverage","Degree of combined leverage","DOL × DFL = %Δ net income ÷ %Δ sales","At the same operating point, degree of operating leverage (DOL) × degree of financial leverage (DFL) gives combined leverage.","Measures how sensitive net income is to a change in sales through operating and financial fixed costs.","DOL = 2.0; DFL = 1.5.\nCombined leverage = 2.0 × 1.5 = 3.0. A 1% sales change implies approximately a 3% net-income change near this point."],
["Equity Investments","Valuation ratios","Justified forward P/E ratio","Dividend payout ratio ÷ (required return − growth rate)","Derived from Gordon growth; required return must exceed perpetual growth. Uses forward earnings.","The P/E multiple implied by constant dividend growth and the required return.","Payout ratio = 40%; required return = 10%; growth = 5%.\nJustified forward P/E = 0.40 ÷ (0.10 − 0.05) = 8×."],
["Equity Investments","Valuation ratios","Book value per share","Common shareholders’ equity ÷ common shares outstanding","Subtract preferred equity when calculating common book value, if applicable.","Accounting common equity attributable to each common share.","Common equity = $600m; common shares = 30m.\nBook value per share = 600 ÷ 30 = $20."],
["Equity Investments","Residual income valuation","Residual income","Net income − (beginning common equity × cost of equity)","Use equity at the start of the period and a cost of equity matched to that period.","Earnings remaining after charging for the opportunity cost of equity capital.","Net income = $50m; beginning common equity = $400m; cost of equity = 10%.\nResidual income = 50 − (400 × 10%) = $10m."],
["Equity Investments","Valuation ratios","Price-to-sales ratio","Share price ÷ sales per share = market capitalization ÷ revenue","Use matching share count and revenue period.","Equity value relative to revenue; particularly useful when earnings are negative, though margins still matter.","Share price = $30; sales per share = $15.\nP/S = 30 ÷ 15 = 2×."],
["Equity Investments","Valuation ratios","Price-to-cash-flow ratio","Share price ÷ operating cash flow per share","Use the cash-flow definition and period specified in the question.","Equity value relative to cash flow per share.","Share price = $30; operating cash flow per share = $3.\nP/CF = 30 ÷ 3 = 10×."],
["Alternative Investments","Real estate","Real estate total return","Income return + capital appreciation return","A basic unlevered return decomposition; use consistent beginning value and period. Income return may use net operating income depending on the question.","Combines property income with change in property value.","Income return = 6%; capital appreciation return = 2%.\nTotal return = 6% + 2% = 8%."],
["Fixed Income","Yield measures","Credit spread","Corporate bond YTM − comparable government bond YTM","Compare yields with similar currency, maturity and relevant benchmark characteristics.","Compensation above the government benchmark, including credit and other spread risks.","Corporate YTM = 6.2%; comparable government YTM = 4.0%.\nCredit spread = 2.2 percentage points, or 220 bps."],
["Fixed Income","Interest rate risk","Macaulay duration","Σ [t × PV(CFₜ)] ÷ bond price","t is measured in coupon periods when yields are per coupon period; convert to years if required. PV cash flows at the bond's yield under the standard calculation.","Present-value-weighted average time to receive the bond's cash flows.","Cash flows: $5 in year 1 and $105 in year 2; yield = 10%; price ≈ $91.32.\nMacaulay duration = [1(5/1.10) + 2(105/1.10²)] ÷ 91.32 ≈ 1.95 years."],
["Fixed Income","Interest rate risk","Modified duration","Macaulay duration ÷ (1 + YTM per coupon period)","For m coupons per year, use YTM/m; ensure duration and yield use matching period units.","Approximate percentage price sensitivity to a small yield change for fixed cash flows.","Macaulay duration = 1.95 years; annual YTM = 10%.\nModified duration = 1.95 ÷ 1.10 ≈ 1.77."],
["Fixed Income","Yield measures","Discount margin (approximation)","Required yield − reference rate","A simplified spread approximation for a floating-rate note; the actual discount margin is the spread over the reference rate that equates the present value of projected payments to the observed price.","Indicates the extra yield over the reference rate required by the investor.","Required yield = 6%; reference rate = 4%.\nApproximate discount margin = 6% − 4% = 2%, or 200 bps."],
["Derivatives","Forward pricing","Current value of a long forward","Vₜ = Sₜ − F₀(T) ÷ (1 + r)^(T − t)","For a non-income-producing underlying with annual compounding; F₀(T) is the original delivery price, Sₜ is current spot, and T − t is remaining time.","Value today of an existing long forward; it is zero at initiation when the contract is fairly priced.","Current spot = $110; original delivery price = $105; risk-free rate = 5%; one year remains.\nVₜ = 110 − 105 ÷ 1.05 = $10."],
["Derivatives","Option valuation","One-period binomial option value","V₀ = [qVᵤ + (1 − q)V_d] ÷ (1 + r)","Vᵤ and V_d are option values in the up/down states; q is the risk-neutral up probability; r is the one-period risk-free rate.","Values an option by risk-neutral expected payoff discounted at the risk-free rate in a one-step model.","Vᵤ = $20; V_d = $0; q = 0.5; r = 5%.\nV₀ = [0.5 × 20 + 0.5 × 0] ÷ 1.05 ≈ $9.52."],
["Derivatives","Option valuation","Risk-neutral up probability","q = [(1 + r) − d] ÷ (u − d)","For a one-period binomial model with no income; u and d are gross up/down multipliers of the underlying price, and r is the risk-free rate for that period.","The probability that makes the underlying's expected return equal the risk-free rate in the binomial pricing model.","u = 1.20; d = 0.90; r = 5%.\nq = (1.05 − 0.90) ÷ (1.20 − 0.90) = 0.50."],
["Derivatives","Option payoffs","Short call payoff at expiration","−max(0, Sₜ − K)","Sₜ is the underlying price at expiration and K is strike; excludes premium.","The call writer's payoff is the opposite of the long call payoff; losses can be substantial if the underlying rises.","Expiration price = $120; strike = $100.\nShort call payoff = −max(0, 120 − 100) = −$20 per unit."],
["Derivatives","Option payoffs","Short put payoff at expiration","−max(0, K − Sₜ)","Sₜ is the underlying price at expiration and K is strike; excludes premium.","The put writer's payoff is the opposite of the long put payoff; losses are limited to the strike if the underlying falls to zero.","Expiration price = $80; strike = $100.\nShort put payoff = −max(0, 100 − 80) = −$20 per unit."],
["Derivatives","Option valuation","Option time value","Option premium − intrinsic value","Intrinsic value is max(0, S₀ − K) for a call and max(0, K − S₀) for a put. For a standard option, premium is at least intrinsic value.","The portion of an option's premium above its immediate exercise value; generally reflects remaining time and uncertainty.","Call premium = $8; spot = $105; strike = $100.\nIntrinsic value = $5; time value = 8 − 5 = $3."],
["Quantitative Methods","Hypothesis testing","Statistical power","1 − β","β is the probability of a Type II error under a specified alternative; power depends on the alternative, sample size, variability and significance level.","Probability of correctly rejecting a false null hypothesis under the specified alternative.","Type II error probability β = 20%.\nPower = 1 − 0.20 = 80%."],
["Portfolio Management","Risk and return","Sharpe ratio","(Portfolio return − risk-free rate) ÷ portfolio standard deviation","Use returns and standard deviation measured over the same period; annualize consistently if comparing annual ratios.","Excess return per unit of total risk; higher is generally preferable for comparable portfolios.","Portfolio return = 10%; risk-free rate = 2%; portfolio standard deviation = 16%.\nSharpe ratio = (10% − 2%) ÷ 16% = 0.50."]
);
